Subject: [PATCH v2 0/3] mm: batch file VMA insertions during fork
To: linux-mm@kvack.org
Cc: linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org,
    linux-kselftest@vger.kernel.org,
    Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>,
    David Hildenbrand <david@kernel.org>,
    Liam R. Howlett <liam@infradead.org>,
    Lorenzo Stoakes <ljs@kernel.org>,
    Shuah Khan <shuah@kernel.org>,
    Mateusz Guzik <mjguzik@gmail.com>

dup_mmap() acquires the file mapping's i_mmap_rwsem separately for each
inherited VMA. This series groups up to eight eligible VMAs for the same
address_space into one insertion critical section. A full batch therefore
uses one lock acquisition instead of eight.

The series first shares the existing page-table copy predicate, then adds
batching and extends the rmap selftest. Pending entries are linked before
page tables are copied and before failed-fork teardown. Open callbacks
retain immediate insertion. No user-visible API changes are introduced.

Changes since the first local revision:
  - Follow the init/add/process/final helper pattern from commit
    3577dbb19241 ("mm: batch unlink_file_vma calls in free_pgd_range").
  - Rename unlink_vma_file_batch to vma_file_batch and share its counted
    VMA array and capacity. The new link_vma_file_batch embeds it and adds
    only the parent VMA pointers needed by insert_after().
  - Use link_file_vma_batch_* names and derive the mapping from the first
    VMA. Preserve unlink batching's layout and grouping rules.
  - Rebuild and rerun the regression tests after this refactoring.

Validation on x86_64 QEMU TCG with KASAN and PROVE_LOCKING:
  - Full kernel build and strict checkpatch: passed.
  - Existing VMA unit tests: 26 passed.
  - New fork/rmap cases: 10 passed; also passed on the baseline previously.
  - Guard-region fork cases: 5 passed, 4 inapplicable variants skipped.
  - 32 forced fork ENOMEM failures: cleanup and write sealing passed.
  - Kprobe tracing: four full batches for 33 same-file VMAs and 32
    seven-entry tail batches during the forced fork failures.
  - No KASAN, lockdep or other kernel diagnostics were observed.

Timing samples from the debug TCG guests were noisy, including the
alternating-file control. They do not establish a latency improvement.
Real-hardware performance measurements and USERFAULTFD runtime coverage
are still outstanding; CONFIG_USERFAULTFD was disabled for these tests.

The batching change is based on Dong Chenchen's supplied prototype.
Codex assisted with adapting it to this tree, adding regression tests and
writing the descriptions, as recorded by the Assisted-by tags.

